{
  "requirement": "Each language's fingerprint rules determine which attachments\ncontribute to the owning item's structural hash.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligation.** `requirements.sdk.outlines.attachments.fingerprint` has one testable obligation. Each language's fingerprint rules decide which attachments contribute to the owning item's structural hash. So the tests must show, per language, that some attachments contribute and others don't.\n\n**Language rules** (from `src/outline/language.rs` `Profile`, `languages/*.rs` and `Builder::encode` in `src/outline/mod.rs`):\n- Rust hashes only doc comments (`hashes_comment: is_doc_comment`).\n- Rust also attaches attributes and outer doc comments to the following item (`fn[attaches]` in `languages/rust.rs`).\n- Python, TypeScript, CSS, Markdown, TOML and YAML set `hashes_comment: never`.\n- Python attaches decorators to the decorated definition (`fn[attaches]` in `languages/python.rs`).\n\n**Rust: contributing attachments.** `fn[hashes_skip_whitespace_and_plain_comments_but_not_doc_comments]`:\n- Changing the doc text (`Doc.` to `Docs.`) changes the hash.\n- Changing the attribute (`inline` to `cold`) changes the hash.\n- Changing the body changes the hash.\n- A parent module's structural hash changes when a child edit is made.\n- The identity hash is unchanged in that parent-module case.\n\n**Rust: non-contributing attachments.**\n- The same test places a plain `// {cite}` comment among the attachments. Replacing it with different text, reflowing the body, and shifting line positions all leave the hash unchanged.\n- `fn[reflowing_inner_and_block_doc_comments_keeps_the_hash]` covers the doc-comment forms: inner `//!`, block `/*! */` and `/** */`, and `///`. Re-wrapped prose keeps the hash, and removing a word changes it. This shows doc comments contribute by their prose, not their delimiters.\n\n**Python.** `fn[python_decorators_attach_to_the_decorated_definition_for_hashing]`:\n- Changing, removing or whitespace-reflowing a decorator behaves correctly: changing or removing it changes the hash, and whitespace changes don't.\n- A method's decorator changes its class's structural hash.\n- That same edit leaves the class's identity hash unchanged.\n\n**Other bundled languages.** `fn[bundled_fingerprints_exclude_comments_and_preserve_word_boundaries]` runs a case for each of Rust, Python, TS, TSX, CSS, Markdown, TOML and YAML.\n- Editing comment text and whitespace leaves the hash unchanged.\n- Joining words changes the hash.\n- Additional assertions check that a Python docstring is hashed, since it is code and not a comment, and that an inline Markdown HTML comment inside prose is hashed.\n- These assertions cover the `never` rule for every non-Rust language.\n\n**Conclusion.** Both outcomes (contributes and does not contribute) are exercised and asserted, for comment and non-comment attachments, in the languages whose rules differ. I found no scenario the requirement names that is left untested.",
  "references": "1c74bf63aa9a7132fdf54df37896eed61aea591d0b7ac84ebf8acf5682020bc0",
